🔹 Part 1 – Small Example (Bell State)

Soch lo tumhare paas 2 qubits hain.
Goal: un dono ko entangle karna (aise jodna ki unki state hamesha linked rahe).

Steps:

Circuit banao

Pehle qubit pe Hadamard lagao → wo superposition me aa jaata hai.

Fir CNOT lagao qubit 0 → qubit 1 par.
👉 Ab dono qubits entangled hain (Bell pair).

Optimize karo

Real quantum hardware me directly circuit nahi chal sakta, kyunki uska apna layout aur connectivity hoti hai.

Isliye transpile karte ho taki circuit hardware ke hisaab se adjust ho jaye.

Run karo

Estimator use karke expectation values nikaalo (matlab operators ke average measurement results).

Analysis

Agar circuit sahi bana, to tumhe ⟨ZZ⟩ aur ⟨XX⟩ dono ka result ~1 milega.

Matlab: qubits entangled hain ✅

🔹 Part 2 – Large Example (100-Qubit GHZ State)

Ab imagine karo tum 100 qubits ek saath entangle karna chahte ho 😅
Ye hai GHZ state (Greenberger–Horne–Zeilinger).

Steps:

Circuit banao

Pehle qubit pe Hadamard lagao.

Fir usko baaki 99 qubits ke saath ek ek karke CNOT se connect karo.
👉 Sabhi qubits ek saath entangled ho jaate hain.

Optimize for hardware

Ab 100 qubits ka circuit bahut bada hai, aur hardware ke layout alag hote hain.

Isliye transpiler fir se layout adjust karta hai.

Run karo IBM ke real backend pe

Simulator me 100 qubits chalana mushkil hai, isliye IBM ke real quantum computer par run karte ho.

Error suppression aur mitigation bhi lagate ho (kyunki noise kaafi hoti hai).

Analysis

Tum check karte ho ki qubit 0 ka correlation baaki 99 qubits ke saath kaisa hai (⟨Z0Zi⟩).

Graph plot karte ho → agar sab theek hai, correlation strong dikhna chahiye (par thoda noise ke wajah se girta hai).

👉 Summary in Hinglish:

Bell state = 2 qubits entangled (easy example).

GHZ state = 100 qubits entangled (utility-scale, real hardware required).

Dono cases me workflow same hai: map → optimize → execute → analyze.🔹 Part 1 – Small Example (Bell State)

